+# Proposals awaiting Justin, from the entropy-guard assessment of 7 October 2026
+
+**None of these is a decision.** Each is a change of intent or of a standing rule that the work has run ahead of, or
+that the assessment recommends. Nothing here is acted on until Justin records his decision in this file, dated, in his
+words. Which record should own decisions is itself open (question Q1); if he chooses another, this file moves there.
+
+## 1. ORC's service adapter launches subprocesses
+
+- **Enacted:** `test/architecture.test.ts` (lines 822-835 in ORC) approves `src/adapters/orc-service.ts` as a fourth
+  subprocess module: git's read commands in ORC's own checkout, `systemctl --user restart orc.service`, and a
+  frozen-lockfile `pnpm install` (orchestrator#101). ORC's `README.md` describes the restart card it serves.
+- **Described:** ORC's `AGENTS.md` (lines 92-96) lists three subprocess modules and not this one.
+- **Authorised:** no decision of Justin's for this authority was visible in either repository. orchestrator#101 may hold
+  one; it was not readable.
+- **Proposal:** Justin records whether this subprocess authority is approved. Only then is the module added to the list
+  in ORC's `AGENTS.md`, citing his decision.
+
+## 2. What ORC's "Deliberately absent" list still holds
+
+- **Described:** ORC's `README.md` (lines 152-154) lists reminders, additional external data sources, workflow execution,
+  sandboxes, shell access and file edits as absent until decided.
+- **Enacted since:** durable work runs declared task types (decided 2026-09-17, `decisions/2026-09-17-async-work-
+  architecture.md`); Scope packages declare their own connectors; ORC's browser reaches the hosts a grant approves
+  (decided 3 Oct, "lets use the library").
+- **Not plain:** whether those decisions cover "workflow execution" and "additional external data sources".
+- **Proposal:** Justin says item by item which are now decided. The README is corrected only for those. Scheduling is
+  already covered by the 2026-09-17 decision and is corrected separately.
+
+## 3. "Retrospective" documentation covers descriptions, not statements of intent
+
+- **The rule:** ORC's `AGENTS.md` (lines 154-155), "Keep documentation concise and retrospective: record only what the
+  implementation and real use established"; also ORC's `README.md` (lines 22-23) and this Scope's `AGENTS.md` (line 29).
+- **The risk:** read literally, it tells an agent to edit a statement of direction, or the "Deliberately absent" list,
+  to match what was built. Proposal 2 is a live case: under this rule an agent would delete "workflow execution" now;
+  under a decision-first reading it waits for Justin.
+- **Proposal:** add one sentence to ORC's `AGENTS.md` "Working Style": "Statements of direction, and of what is
+  deliberately absent, change only on Justin's recorded decision."
+
+## 4. Where credentials are read
+
+- **Described:** ORC's `README.md` (line 103): "A credential is read in exactly one place, `src/runtime.ts`". ORC's
+  `SECURITY-REVIEW.md` (line 120) holds "one place that reads a credential" as the standard for new work.
+- **Enacted:** a Scope's credentials are read from that Scope's own folder by `src/adapters/scope-credentials.ts`, and
+  ORC's web token by `src/web-cli.ts` (line 625).
+- **Authorised:** the operator's decision of 28 Sep 2026 that Scope logins are not centralised
+  (`src/adapters/scope-credentials.ts`, line 14) covers the Scope credentials. Nothing visible covers the web token.
+- **Proposal:** Justin says what "one place" now means: one place per owner (ORC's own settings, each Scope's folder),
+  or literally one module. The README and the standard are then corrected to match his decision, or the web token's
+  read moves.
